WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.640
David, do you think vaccination phases&nbsp;
are ethical once you get to vaccinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.640
health care workers? Is it really ethical to&nbsp;
limit who can get the vaccine? Beyond that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.280
I mean, listen, this is basic utilitarian&nbsp;
utilitarianism. It's triage. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.800
try to reduce the amount of death and&nbsp;
people who get seriously ill from covid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:32.640
if that's the goal, if we all agree that those&nbsp;
statistically more likely to get a cold flu like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.480
are in one group and those statistically more&nbsp;
likely to die from covid are in another group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.760
it's completely utilitarian to say they get&nbsp;
the vaccine first. So you would start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.320
the oldest folks and those with the most serious&nbsp;
medical conditions. There's nothing particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:57.440
unique about that. It's simply utilitarianism.&nbsp;
Now, where it can get tricky is the practice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.080
figuring out where some of these lines are.&nbsp;
So, for example, throughout the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.720
grocery store workers have been essential,&nbsp;
essential workers. So we've heard the term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:15.760
but they've not been prioritized. We know that so&nbsp;
many grocery store workers have gotten sick with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.880
the virus and we're saying they're essential, but&nbsp;
they're not being prioritized. Maybe it's being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:27.040
done incorrectly. But the idea of figuring out at&nbsp;
least when there's limited supply who can get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:35.040
vaccine first is perfectly utilitarian. Now, you&nbsp;
can look at other criteria. You could say, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.400
we want this to be egalitarian and so we're&nbsp;
going to do it by lottery. As an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.560
you could make the case that that's preferable&nbsp;
in some way. It's not likely to reduce death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.960
The studies that I've looked at do say that&nbsp;
starting with the oldest and the sickest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.600
is the way to reduce that death. And in fact,&nbsp;
we're seeing it. If you look at the numbers now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.840
cases have sort of plateaued. They've&nbsp;
plateaued right around 50000 today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.520
And hospitalizations keep declining and&nbsp;
deaths keep declining. Now, the deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.320
are on a delay from cases. So we'll have to&nbsp;
continue to watch that. But that makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.200
because you you tend to have people behave in a&nbsp;
way that's reflective of the risk they perceive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.880
There are definitely younger people. As we&nbsp;
now learn about more and more older folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.160
being vaccinated, there are more younger&nbsp;
people doing whatever the hell they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:31.600
spreading covid. It's leading to cases, but the&nbsp;
deaths and hospitalizations are going down because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.440
the most vulnerable have already been vaccinated.&nbsp;
So I do believe that as vaccine supply increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.720
the restrictions probably become I don't know&nbsp;
if the word is less ethical, but less necessary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.480
And certainly any thought vaccine should be used&nbsp;
no matter what. There are some anecdotal stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.600
and we've covered some of them of people&nbsp;
who have been penalized and in some cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:02.240
I believe, even fired for using open doses,&nbsp;
which would go bad if you leave them overnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.200
on people who did not yet meet criteria. I think&nbsp;
that's terrible. And to the extent that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.600
happening anecdotally, it shouldn't. It's very&nbsp;
bad people. We should be using every single dose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.360
A dose in a trash can is never better, no matter&nbsp;
what, than a dose in someone's arm. But yes, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.880
a utilitarian standpoint, and this is a common way&nbsp;
to analyze some of these medical ethics issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:30.640
there's nothing unethical about the vaccine&nbsp;
triage. And it does appear as though it's being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.600
done more or less in the right way. But there&nbsp;
are arguments to be made about, well, teachers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.920
maybe should have been earlier if they're going&nbsp;
to have to be in schools with kids, if grocery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.120
workers really are essential, we know a lot of&nbsp;
them are getting sick. They should be on. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.840
you can tweak with with some of the specifics,&nbsp;
but there's nothing unethical in principal.

